course: dictionary
lesson: 5821
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, so you must read the words around them to know which one is used. «Many birds, like magpies, swoop at people near their nest in spring.» The words birds and nest tell you a magpie is an animal here, and near their nest in spring tells you why it swoops.
explain: A magpie is a black-and-white bird with a long tail and a loud chattering call. In informal talk, a magpie can also be a person who likes to collect small shiny or odd things. To choose the right meaning, swap it into the sentence: «The magpie sat on the fence and chattered loudly» only makes sense with the bird.
explain: Stories can also shape how we use a word. Old stories say magpies steal shiny things, which is why a collector is sometimes called a magpie. Studies show the bird does not really love shiny objects, so the story is not the whole truth. Always check a meaning against the facts, not only against what a story says.
fact: «Magpie» means a black-and-white bird with a long tail and a loud chattering call.
fact: Magpies are clever birds of the crow family.
fact: «Swoop» means to dive down fast at something.
fact: In informal talk, a magpie can be a person who likes to collect small shiny or odd things.
fact: «One for sorrow, two for joy» is a counting rhyme about magpies.
exercise: «Many birds, like magpies, swoop at people near their nest in spring.» What does swoop mean? | Sing a sweet song | *Dive down fast at something | Fly slowly away | Build a nest from twigs
exercise: «The magpie sat on the fence and chattered loudly.» Which meaning of magpie is used here? | A loud school bell | A person who likes to collect small shiny things | A kind of tree with white bark | *A black-and-white bird with a long tail
exercise: «Australian magpies swoop people in spring to protect their nests.» Find and copy one word that means keep safe. | swoop | nests | spring | *protect
test: What is the best thing to do if a magpie swoops near its nest? | Throw food at the bird | Run and wave your arms about | *Cover your head and walk calmly away | Climb the tree to look at the nest
test: «Parents of every kind defend their young.» What does defend mean? | Feed | Attack | Hide | *Guard and protect
exam: «The magpie sat on the fence and chattered loudly.» Mia says magpie means a person who collects shiny things. Is she right? | Yes, because the bird is shiny | *No, here it means the black-and-white bird | No, here it means a fence post | Yes, because chattering is what collectors do
